---
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const baseUrl = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : rawBase + '/';

const predicted = [
  {
    num: '3.1 %',
    label: 'predicted per-drop MAPE, pooled across 20 / 50 / 100 mL·h⁻¹.',
    tone: 'flow',
  },
  {
    num: '±3.87 µL',
    label: '95 % Limits of Agreement on 50 µL drops, committed to git before any bench data exists.',
    tone: 'flow',
  },
  {
    num: '49 %',
    label: 'of total predicted error attributable to print tolerance on the sensor arm — the dominant Rev-C lever.',
    tone: 'ir',
  },
  {
    num: '2.7 %',
    label: 'V_cal uncertainty at CAL_N = 10 (35 s of calibration time at 50 mL·h⁻¹) — drip-set-CV-limited.',
    tone: 'flow',
  },
];

---

<section id="analysis">
  <div class="eyebrow">Analysis</div>
  <h2>Predicted accuracy from first principles, before any bench data.</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    A Monte Carlo error budget propagates measurement uncertainty through the
    firmware's volume-inversion math to predict per-drop MAPE, Bland-Altman
    Limits of Agreement, and calibration convergence — committed to git
    <em>before</em> the validation campaign began. The four headline
    numbers below are the pre-bench prediction; the
    <a href="#limitations">Limitations</a> section carries the measured
    cross-session Bland-Altman so the predict-vs-measure gap is reviewable
    side-by-side. Source notebook:
    <code>analysis/notebooks/error_budget.ipynb</code>.
  </p>

  <div class="analysis-stats">
    {predicted.map((s) => (
      <div class={`stat tone-${s.tone}`}>
        <div class="num">{s.num}</div>
        <div class="label">{s.label}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="lead">
    These four numbers were the pre-bench prediction. The MC budget
    correctly identified print tolerance as the dominant predicted source
    (1.54 percentage points of the 3.13 % MAPE) but <em>under-estimated
    the residual ~6×</em>; the gap turns out to be the position-dependent
    contamination of the chord measurement, visible directly in the raw
    photodiode waveforms below. The capture firmware
    (<code>ENABLE_RAW_CAPTURE</code> on the
    <code>feat/raw-capture-rev-c</code> branch) is <em>not</em> the Rev-B
    submission firmware; submission firmware = HEAD of <code>v2</code>.
    See <a href="#limitations">§17 in Limitations</a> for the full
    architectural treatment.
  </p>

  <figure class="analysis-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/raw_combined_P3_minus4mm.png`}
         alt="TOP and BOT beam-occlusion waveforms overlaid in a single panel for 23 consecutive drops at mount position P3 (4 mm below canonical). TOP curves (blue) peak around +2 ms after the trigger anchor with a small secondary shoulder approaching from negative time. BOT curves (orange) peak around +11 ms with a bifurcated structure showing a second peak at +14 ms." />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>One drop, both beams, one chart.</strong> 23 drops at
      <code>P3 (-4 mm)</code>, TOP (blue) and BOT (orange) overlaid in
      the same panel. The ~11 ms transit gap between TOP firing and BOT
      firing is the dual-beam architecture's velocity signal — directly
      visible. The two position-dependent contaminants of the chord
      measurement are also direct-readable: <em>(i)</em> the TOP shoulder
      approaching the trigger around <code>−2 ms</code> is the
      <strong>umbilical</strong> — the fluid filament still tethering the
      drop to the chamber tip; <em>(ii)</em> the bifurcated BOT peak
      around <code>+11 ms / +14 ms</code> is the <strong>backsplash</strong>
      trailing edge — particles crossing BOT after the drop body has
      cleared.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="analysis-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/raw_median_bands_all_positions.png`}
         alt="Median trace and 25 to 75 percent IQR band for each of three mount positions, TOP and BOT beams shown in separate panels. TOP medians overlap closely across all three positions. BOT medians shift systematically in timing — P1 canonical mount peaks first, P3 minus 4 mm peaks last with a visible second hump." />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>What position-dependence actually looks like.</strong>
      Median trace (line) + 25–75 % IQR band (shaded) per mount position,
      both beams. TOP shapes are largely <strong>position-invariant</strong>
      — the medians overlap closely across P1, P2, P3.
      BOT timing shifts <strong>systematically</strong> with mount
      geometry: P1 canonical peaks ~10 ms after trigger, P3 (-4 mm) peaks
      ~12 ms after trigger with a visible second hump. The
      <a href="#limitations">§17</a> finding is that this BOT-shape
      variation is exactly what defeats a position-invariant chord-time
      → volume estimate. Bench n_drops: P1=40, P2=22, P3=23.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure class="analysis-figure">
    <img src={`${baseUrl}analysis/raw_overlays_P1_high.png`}
         alt="Two-panel overlay of 40 drops at the canonical V_50_05-era mount position. Left panel TOP beam peaks consistently around 225 to 260 ADC counts. Right panel BOT beam peaks 397 to 508 ADC counts with a small late-time hump visible after most drops." />
    <figcaption>
      <strong>40 drops at the canonical mount</strong> — the densest
      single-mount capture in the campaign. Per-channel peak ranges are
      tight (TOP 225–260, BOT 397–508; ±7 % and ±12 % around the median),
      meaning the device's per-drop response is repeatable; what makes
      <code>V_CAL_K = 1.27</code> hold at <em>this</em> mount within
      ±30 % is the same data driving the predicted accuracy envelope
      above. Move the device 4 mm down the chamber (P3 above) and the
      BOT-shape family changes — that's what
      <a href="#limitations">§17</a> is about.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="reproducibility">
    <div class="repro-label">Reproducibility</div>
    <p>
      <strong>Synthetic error budget.</strong> The figure regenerates byte-identical from a fresh clone — no input data
      required. Fixed RNG seed (<code>20260513</code>, the bench date) keeps
      the prediction stable across re-runs until a parameter changes. The
      assumption table for every input is the docstring of
      <code>scripts.error_budget.default_params</code>; every value traces to
      a measurement, a datasheet, or a flagged placeholder.
    </p>
    <pre><code>cd analysis
docker compose up regenerate-error-budget</code></pre>

    <p style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <strong>Validation pipeline (sanity-check figures + Bland-Altman).</strong> The same Dockerfile rebuilds the validation notebook against either the committed sample subset (<code>data/sample/</code>) — the cold-clone test — or the full bench dataset (<code>data/raw/</code>):
    </p>
    <pre><code>cd analysis
docker compose up regenerate-figures-sample   # cold-clone test (sample data)
docker compose up regenerate-figures          # full campaign data</code></pre>

    <p class="repro-foot">
      <strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Docker + git installed locally. First build downloads ~300 MB of pinned Python + scientific-stack and takes ~3 min on a typical laptop. Pipeline source: <code>analysis/notebooks/validation.ipynb</code>. Dependencies hash-locked in <code>analysis/requirements.txt</code> (Python 3.12.7, <code>pip&nbsp;--require-hashes</code>). The same two commands run on every push to <code>v2</code> in CI (<code>.github/workflows/build-and-test.yml</code>) on a fresh ubuntu-latest runner — green CI is the cold-clone reproducibility proof.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  #analysis h2 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 40px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    max-width: 22ch;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  #analysis h3 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 22px;
    line-height: 1.25;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  #analysis em { color: var(--ir); font-style: normal; }

  .analysis-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 40px 0 56px;
  }
  .analysis-stats .stat {
    padding: 20px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .analysis-stats .stat .num {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 34px;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .analysis-stats .stat .label {
    font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  .analysis-stats .stat.tone-ir .num { color: var(--ir); }
  .analysis-stats .stat.tone-flow .num { color: var(--flow); }

  .analysis-figure {
    margin: 0 0 56px;
    background: #ffffff;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: 18px;
  }
  .analysis-figure img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .analysis-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 14px;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .analysis-figure figcaption code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    background: var(--bg-3);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  h3.findings-heading {
    font-size: 28px;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  .findings {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 56px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
  }
  .findings li {
    padding: 18px 20px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ir);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
  }
  .findings li strong {
    display: block;
    color: var(--fg);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .findings li span {
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .reproducibility {
    padding: 24px;
    background: var(--bg-3);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .repro-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    color: var(--flow);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .reproducibility p {
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0 0 14px;
  }
  .reproducibility p code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--fg);
    background: var(--bg-2);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .reproducibility pre {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 14px 18px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 6px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .reproducibility p.repro-foot {
    margin-top: 22px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .reproducibility p.repro-foot strong { color: var(--fg-dim); }
  .reproducibility p.repro-foot code { font-size: 12px; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .analysis-stats { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 540px) {
    .analysis-stats { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    #analysis h2 { font-size: 32px; }
  }
</style>
